                "event": "Flash Flood Warning",
                "sender": "w-nws.webmaster@noaa.gov",
                "senderName": "NWS Raleigh NC",
                "headline": "Flash Flood Warning issued September 4 at 7:49PM EDT until September 4 at 10:45PM EDT by NWS Raleigh NC",
                "description": "FFWRAH\n\nThe National Weather Service in Raleigh has issued a\n\n* Flash Flood Warning for...\nNortheastern Edgecombe County in central North Carolina...\nHalifax County in central North Carolina...\n\n* Until 1045 PM EDT.\n\n* At 749 PM EDT, Doppler radar indicated thunderstorms producing\nheavy rain across the warned area. Between 1 and 2 inches of rain\nhave fallen. Additional rainfall amounts of 2 to 3 inches are\npossible in the warned area. Flash flooding is ongoing or expected\nto begin shortly.\n\nHAZARD...Flash flooding caused by thunderstorms.\n\nSOURCE...Radar indicated.\n\nIMPACT...Flash flooding of small creeks and streams, urban areas,\nhighways, streets and underpasses as well as other poor\ndrainage and low-lying areas.\n\n* Some locations that will experience flash flooding include...\nHalifax, Scotland Neck, Enfield, Weldon, Hobgood, Tillery, South\nWeldon and Medoc Mountain State Park.\n\nThis includes the following frequently flooded locations...\nUS Hwy 301 near Burnt Coat Swamp and Beech Swamp, Thirteen Bridges Rd\nnear Beech Swamp, NC Hwy 125 near Conoconnara Swamp and Hwy 258\nbetween E 16th St and Lees Meadow Rd.",
                "instruction": "Turn around, don't drown when encountering flooded roads. Most flood\ndeaths occur in vehicles.",
                "response": "Avoid",
                "note": null,
                "parameters": {
                    "AWIPSidentifier": [
                        "FFWRAH"
                    ],
                    "WMOidentifier": [
                        "WGUS52 KRAH 042349"
                    ],
